{
  "statement": "Traditional negligence law cannot protect citizens from automated failures because plaintiffs cannot prove what happens inside a proprietary neural network. Under current rules, tech giants write terms of service that shift all legal risk to local users. For example, Epic Systems integrated OpenAI's software into health records in 2023, but the contract shields the developer from medical malpractice suits. State courts must apply strict product liability to upstream developers. When a black box causes a localized disaster, the victim cannot trace the code. Strict liability bypasses this evidentiary wall by focusing on the harm rather than the developer's internal process.\n\nRegulators must treat foundational models as physical component parts under established product liability standards. Consider how the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration forced Takata to pay billions for exploding airbag inflators, rather than blaming the car brands. Upstream AI developers control the compute, the training data, and the system architecture. They are the only actors who can engineer safety into the system. Shifting liability to the small-scale downstream deployer—like a local clinic or a municipal transit authority—creates a moral hazard. If the core model is defective, the upstream creator must pay for the damage.\n\nComplex compliance standards do not make systems safer; they simply allow dominant tech firms to pull up the drawbridge against smaller competitors. The European Union's AI Act imposes third-party conformity assessments that cost upwards of $300,000 per system. Microsoft can write that check without noticing. A five-person startup in Ohio cannot. This dynamic mirrors the aftermath of the 2018 General Data Protection Regulation, which increased market concentration for dominant tech firms by 17 percent as small competitors folded. If policymakers want safety and competition, they must ditch complex bureaucratic checklists and enforce a simple rule: if your system breaks and hurts someone, you pay. Who actually benefits when we replace clear liability with a thicket of government certifications?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "🔌",
      "tooltip": "A software model is just an industrial component plugged into a larger machine, and the supplier must be held liable when the connection fails."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🏰",
      "tooltip": "Complex compliance audits are not safety barriers; they are regulatory moats designed to keep small competitors out of the market."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "State courts must apply strict product liability to upstream developers.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": "headline"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Epic Systems integrated OpenAI's software into health records in 2023, but the contract shields the developer from medical malpractice suits.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": "evidence"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Regulators must treat foundational models as physical component parts under established product liability standards.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": "premise"
    },
    {
      "claim": "This dynamic mirrors the aftermath of the 2018 General Data Protection Regulation, which increased market concentration for dominant tech firms by 17 percent as small competitors folded.",
      "targets": [],
      "relationship": "evidence"
    }
  ]
}

