  "statement": "The claim that strict liability entrenches tech monopolies is a corporate myth designed to shield massive capital. The physical reality of compute concentration has already locked out small players from building frontier models. Look at OpenAI. According to the study \"Constructing AI Speech,\" OpenAI had to abandon its nonprofit structure for a capped-profit model with Microsoft just to survive. Startups do not build these massive systems from scratch because they cannot afford the chips or the power grids. Strict liability targets the well-capitalized infrastructure giants who actually deploy these systems. It forces them to internalize their risks rather than dumping them on the public. State courts and the Federal Trade Commission can enforce this through existing product liability laws. A coalition of small businesses and consumer advocates supports this. Tech monopolists and venture capitalists oppose it.\n\nThe strongest argument against this is that startups operate on razor-thin margins and cannot absorb the unpredictable legal risks of black-box failures, which would dry up venture capital. That financial risk is real and uncomfortable. But complex compliance standards act as the true regressive barriers, letting incumbents pull up the drawbridge, while simple strict liability rules bypass this red tape. Under strict liability, there are no 500-page compliance audits or pre-deployment certification boards to fund. Startups simply buy pre-tested components from upstream giants. If the upstream component fails, the giant pays. This shifts the risk back up the supply chain. We see this in the automotive industry. Small mechanics are not liable for design defects in Bosch braking systems. This argument assumes that insurance markets can accurately price upstream component risks and that compute concentration remains high. Policymakers must realize that simple liability rules protect small competitors by forcing upstream giants to sell safe, insured parts.\n\nDynamic safe harbors based on telemetry and 24-hour patching are a shell game. This exploits the causal uncertainty of shared control to shift blame downstream. If a system fails, the giant will point to their data logs and claim the downstream user missed a patch. These performative measures allow companies to systematically evade effective regulation. Small businesses cannot monitor and apply patches to complex neural networks every hour of the day. If policymakers adopt these safe harbors, they hand tech giants a get-out-of-jail-free card. Local hospitals and small businesses will carry the financial ruins of automated failures. Who pays when the patch itself breaks the system?",
