ured parts.\n\nDynamic safe harbors based on telemetry and 24-hour patching are a shell game. This exploits the causal uncertainty of shared control to shift blame downstream. If a system fails, the giant will point to their data logs and claim the downstream user missed a patch. These performative measures allow companies to systematically evade effective regulation. Small businesses cannot monitor and apply patches to complex neural networks every hour of the day. If policymakers adopt these safe harbors, they hand tech giants a get-out-of-jail-free card. Local hospitals and small businesses will carry the financial ruins of automated failures. Who pays when the patch itself breaks the system?",
