{
  "statement": "Skeptics argue that strict liability creates a clean, market-based incentive. They believe it bypasses slow regulatory agencies by forcing developers to internalize the costs of their failures. However, strict liability does not force upstream tech giants to absorb risk. Upstream platforms use Acceptable Use Policies and downstream indemnification clauses to push all legal liability onto small developers. When a startup deploys an autonomous medical agent, the platform's terms of service force the startup to assume all legal defense costs. If we impose strict liability, big tech will raise API prices or block high-risk use cases entirely. Global private investment in AI reached $150.79 billion in 2024 according to \"Introduction: The Economics of Transformative AI\". Strict liability will lock this capital inside a few mega-corporations that can afford self-insurance. Therefore, strict liability acts as a protectionist barrier for incumbents, not a safety tool.\n\nThe economic reality is worse: strict liability creates an un-underwritable insurance barrier that bans startup competition. Actuarial markets cannot price novel, dynamic software risks without historical loss data. Insurance companies charge massive, flat-fee premiums to cover unquantifiable autonomous system risks. A small developer cannot pay a $500,000 annual premium just to launch a localized autonomous delivery pilot. This burden operates exactly like a regressive regulatory compliance audit. It starves small labs of capital while letting trillion-dollar giants monopolize the market. Consequently, strict liability shuts the door on decentralized innovation by turning insurance into an invisible compliance gate.\n\nSafetyists argue that post-hoc patching is fundamentally insufficient for physical systems. They believe that once an autonomous vehicle or surgical robot fails physically, the damage is done and cannot be undone by a software update. This panic over physical failures relies on a false premise. Post-hoc patching and dynamic safety architectures prevent catastrophic physical failures more effectively than pre-deployment bans. A layered adaptive safety architecture uses real-time telemetry and automated syntactic verification to contain harms before they escalate. Modern autonomous systems do not fail in single, uncatchable leaps. Telemetry monitors system health and shuts down anomalous physical actions in milliseconds, long before a drone or a surgical arm causes irreversible damage. Dynamic safety frameworks allow continuous, real-time updates that patch vulnerabilities within hours of detection. Thus, real-time telemetry and adaptive safety architectures prove that physical harms are containable without freezing deployment.\n\nNow scale that to the human cost: delaying autonomous deployment to avoid localized risks carries a massive, unmeasured human toll. The opportunity cost of technological stagnation is measured in human lives lost to preventable errors. Medical errors kill over 250,000 people annually in the United States alone. Autonomous diagnostic systems and robotic surgical assistants can slash this number immediately. When regulators freeze deployment over hypothetical physical failures, they choose to let real patients die today. Precautionary delay is not safety; it is a passive moral atrocity that protects a broken status quo.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Strict liability is not a neutral scale; it is a weapon used by upstream giants to crush downstream startups through contract shifting."},
    {"symbol": "⏱️", "tooltip": "Real-time telemetry and dynamic patching contain physical risks instantly, exposing the massive human toll of precautionary delays."}
  ],
