{
  "statement": "Congress must pass statutory strict liability laws that void upstream indemnification clauses for high-risk autonomous systems. Opponents argue that upstream developers will use market dominance to contractually shift all liability to small downstream innovators through standard terms of service. Indeed, Epic Systems integrated OpenAI software into health records in 2023 under contracts that shielded the upstream developer from medical malpractice lawsuits. However, the Federal Trade Commission can enforce rules that declare these liability-shifting clauses void as a matter of public policy. Historically, courts have voided private contracts that attempt to waive liability for ultra-hazardous activities like commercial aviation or toxic waste disposal. A bipartisan coalition of small business alliances and consumer groups supports this statutory override, while major technology platforms lobby heavily against it. Without this statutory intervention, upstream monopolists will continue to externalize systemic risks onto small businesses that lack the capital to survive a single catastrophic lawsuit.\n\nReal-time telemetry cannot prevent catastrophic failures because deep neural networks fail confidently when operating outside their training distributions. The paper \"Concrete Problems in AI Safety\" demonstrates that machine learning systems exhibit poor performance on out-of-distribution data while remaining highly confident in their erroneous outputs. A monitoring system cannot flag or halt a failure that the underlying model itself classifies as correct. The AI Incident Database recorded 362 incidents in 2025, up from 233 in 2024, according to the \"AI Index Report 2026: Chapter 3 Responsible AI\". These rising numbers prove that real-time telemetry fails to catch novel failure modes before they cause physical or economic harm. This technical reality forces us to rely on a key assumption: we must assume that out-of-distribution environments are inevitably encountered in real-world deployments. If regulators rely on post-hoc telemetry rather than ex-ante safety gates, they permit systems to fail in the wild before attempting a cure.\n\nThe positive outcomes of post-hoc patching are real — and that's exactly the problem, because no one is accountable for ensuring safety in physical systems where failures are irreversible. We assume that physical safety-critical systems require a fundamentally different regulatory standard than digital-only applications. The Federal Aviation Administration enforces strict ex-ante certification for autopilot systems because a mid-air collision cannot be undone. If an autonomous vehicle collides with a pedestrian, an over-the-air software patch cannot undo the loss of life. The Department of Transportation must enforce mandatory safety-gated deployment protocols before any autonomous vehicle enters public roads. If we allow developers to substitute real-time telemetry for proven ex-ante safety gates, we accept a world where the first warning of a systemic defect is a fatal collision.",
