  "statement": "Treating dynamic software like physical steel beams under the common-law doctrine of non-delegable duty is a disastrous category error. The Skeptic rightly seeks to protect victims by targeting deep-pocketed upstream developers. But this physical-world analogy ignores the diffuse, statistical benefits of general-purpose software. If courts hold upstream developers strictly liable for every downstream integration, open-source AI dies immediately. Meta and Microsoft will close their model weights to shield themselves from unpredictable liability. This legal shift forces a closed-source monopoly. This argument assumes that upstream developers will restrict access rather than pricing in unpredictable risk. It locks out the smaller developers who drove the record $150.79 billion in global private investment in 2024, as documented in \"Introduction: The Economics of Transformative AI\".\n\nThe Safetyist claims that real-time telemetry cannot prevent physical harms because neural networks fail confidently when operating out-of-distribution. This claim assumes safety requires perfect internal model inspection. It does not. A Layered Adaptive Safety Architecture achieves safety by applying runtime verification to model outputs. Engineers do not need to predict every non-linear neural pathway. They only need to block unsafe actions before execution. Think of a digital circuit breaker. The Federal Aviation Administration uses similar boundary-monitoring protocols to manage complex airspace. By shifting focus from internal model state to external output verification, developers can stop physical failures in real time.\n\nForcing developers into endless litigation over shared control will paralyze the economy. Autonomous agents will soon orchestrate transactions across the $20 trillion global e-commerce market, as detailed in \"AI Agents and the Law\". Instead of crushing innovation with adversarial lawsuits, Congress should establish a federal no-fault AI compensation fund. A micro-levy on industrial compute clusters would fund this program. The Federal Emergency Management Agency uses a similar structure for national flood insurance. This framework protects victims without blocking market entry. A coalition of startup founders and consumer advocates supports this approach. It bypasses trial lawyers while keeping open-source models accessible to everyone.",
